    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(5) where α˜p,m,m′ = αp,m,m′ βp,m,m′ is a complex scalar coefficient, with αp,m,m′ the target reflectivity, or radar crosssection (RCS), and βp,m,m′ the product of the path loss from transmit AP m′ to the target at position p and that from the target to receive AP m. As previously mentioned, we follow the Swerling-I model for the RCS, in which αp,m,m′ is kept constant over consecutive symbols within the coherence block [11]. Moreover, φm,p,θm,p are the azimuth and elevation angle of the position p with respect to (w.r.t.) the antenna array of AP m. A similar meaning have the quantities φm′,p and θm′,p w.r.t. AP m′."
